```

So the mechanism, as far as the response evidence shows, is:

1. It is a method on `*Command` meant to be called on the root command (same "InitDefault*" pattern as `InitDefaultHelpCmd`, command.go:1229, which is another of the seeds for this query).
2. It first bails out (no-op) if `c.CompletionOptions.DisableDefaultCmd` is set, or if `!c.HasSubCommands()` — a root command with no subcommands gets no completion command. This call to `HasSubCommands` (command.go:1556) is a confirmed graph edge at completions.go:684 (evidence e47/e48).
3. It then iterates `c.commands` and checks each existing command's `Name()` (command.go:1496, confirmed edge at completions.go:689, evidence e6-e17) and `HasAlias(...)` against `compCmdName`, to detect whether the program already registered its own `completion` command — in which case the default one is skipped (per the doc comment's point 3).

What the response does NOT contain: the snippet for `InitDefaultCompletionCmd` is truncated at line 689 (mid-loop), so the actual `c.AddCommand(...)` call that performs the registration is not shown in source text. Likewise, no snippet in the response shows the call site that invokes `InitDefaultCompletionCmd()` on the root command (e.g., from inside `ExecuteC`, command.go:1052) — only bare line-number relation entries exist for that region of command.go (e.g. e147/e148 at command.go:1063-1064, e73/e85 at 1098-1099, e101 at 1120) without accompanying snippet text, so I can't quote or confirm the exact triggering call.
